🧠 Conceptual Theory
1. What is a Smart Contract?

A smart contract is a self-executing program stored on a blockchain that runs when predefined conditions are met.
They automate execution of agreements, ensuring all participants are immediately certain of the outcome — without intermediaries.

Smart contracts are written in Solidity (for Ethereum-like blockchains).

They are deployed on Ethereum Test Networks such as Rinkeby, Goerli, Sepolia, etc.

2. Purpose of the Practical

This program simulates a bank account using a smart contract:

Allows a customer to deposit and withdraw funds.

Maintains a balance on the blockchain (immutable and transparent).

Demonstrates state management, transactional control, and access restriction (security via msg.sender).

🧩 Concepts Used
a) Blockchain Basics

Blockchain is a distributed ledger of transactions.

Each block stores a hash, timestamp, and data.

Data once written cannot be altered → ensures immutability.

b) Ethereum and Solidity

Ethereum is a blockchain platform supporting smart contracts.

Solidity is an object-oriented programming language for writing them.

Contracts are deployed on Ethereum nodes, and users interact with them via transactions.

c) Key Solidity Concepts in the Practical
Concept	Description
pragma solidity ^x.x.x	Defines compiler version.
contract	Defines the smart contract structure.
address	Represents Ethereum account (user or contract).
msg.sender	The account address calling the contract function.
payable	Allows a function or address to send/receive Ether.
mapping(address => uint)	Stores key-value pairs; here, it maps a user’s address to their balance.
require()	Used for input validation and error handling.
public / private	Access specifiers.
view	Function that only reads data, doesn’t modify state.
d) Test Network

Smart contracts are first deployed on test networks (like Sepolia) instead of the main Ethereum network, to avoid spending real Ether.

You can use:

Remix IDE (online Solidity editor)

MetaMask wallet (to connect to test network)

Test Ether (fake Ether for testing)

⚙️ Algorithm / Working Steps

Start

Define a contract Bank.

Create a mapping balance to store users’ balances.

Implement function deposit():

Accept Ether using payable.

Add to balance[msg.sender].

Implement function withdraw(uint amount):

Check if balance[msg.sender] >= amount.

Subtract and transfer amount.

Implement function getBalance():

Return balance[msg.sender].

Deploy the contract on a test network using Remix.

Interact with the functions via MetaMask.

⏱️ Complexity Analysis
Operation	Time Complexity	Space Complexity	Explanation
Deposit	O(1)	O(n)	Constant time update; storage grows with number of users.
Withdraw	O(1)	O(n)	Constant time check and update.
Show Balance	O(1)	O(n)	Reads mapping in constant time.
